# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: with `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default, and what
# this repo was cloned with) Git rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout.  For ordinary
# source that is harmless.  For the evaluation artifacts under `data/` it is
# not: their *bytes* are the evidence.  Each policy file is pinned by a
# `.sha256` sidecar, and qrels/queries bytes feed `qrels_hash` and the
# provenance claims in `split-manifest.v1.json`.
#
# Left unguarded, a fresh clone on Windows fails 78 tests with
# `POLICY_HASH_MISMATCH` — which reads as "the policy was tampered with" when
# the truth is "Git changed the line endings on checkout".  That is the same
# defect shape this project keeps finding: an infrastructure fault wearing a
# business verdict's clothes.  Marking the artifacts `-text` makes Git store and
# emit them byte-for-byte, so the hash pin means what it claims to mean.

# Evaluation data artifacts: byte-pinned evidence, never line-ending converted.
data/**/*.json    -text
data/**/*.jsonl   -text
data/**/*.sha256  -text
data/**/*.rc      -text

# Test fixtures compared or hashed as bytes (e.g. MinerU middle.json).
tests/fixtures/**/*.json  -text
tests/fixtures/**/*.jsonl -text
tests/fixtures/**/*.pdf   -text

# Normalize source to LF in the repository; checkout may localise it freely.
*.py   text eol=lf
*.go   text eol=lf
*.md   text
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.proto text eol=lf
